<para id="RM0000014C700DX" category="C" type-id="3008X" name-id="0659Y-00" from="200612" to="200912">
<dtccode>P0136/27</dtccode>
<dtcname>O2 Sensor CircuitB1S2</dtcname>
<subpara id="RM0000014C700DX_06" type-id="31" category="03" proc-id="RM0550J___00007IH00000">
<name>Circuit Description</name>
<content5 releasenbr="1">
<list1 type="nonmark">
<item>
<ptxt>A three-way catalytic converter is used to efficiently purify the CO, HC and NOx in the exhaust gas. This three-way catalytic converter functions most efficiently near the stoichiometric air-fuel ratio. For this reason, CO, HC and NOx cannot be efficiently purified unless the engine is accurately controlled near the stoichiometric air-fuel ratio. The oxygen sensor is installed for the purpose of this control. Utilizing the characteristic that its output voltage changes suddenly when it detects the oxygen concentration in the exhaust gas discharged when normal combustion occurs near the stoichiometric air-fuel ratio, it sends signals to the engine control computer so that the air-fuel ratio can be accurately controlled. When the air-fuel ratio becomes lean, the oxygen concentration in the exhaust gas increases, and the oxygen sensor informs the engine control computer that the air-fuel ratio is lean. (Low voltage output: &lt;0.45V) When the air-fuel ratio becomes rich, the oxygen concentration in the exhaust gas decreases, and the oxygen sensor informs the engine control computer that the air-fuel ratio is rich. (High voltage output: &gt;0.45V) The engine control computer judges whether the air-fuel ratio is rich or lean based on the electromotive force signal of the oxygen sensor, and controls the injection volume accordingly. A zirconium element is used for the sensor element. The zirconium element is made of platinum-plated zirconia and includes an integrated heating element. The inside of the zirconium element is exposed to the outside air and the outside is exposed to the exhaust gas, and it generates a small voltage when there is a large difference in oxygen concentration between the exhaust gas and the outside air. When heated, the sensor becomes very efficient. When the exhaust temperature is low, the sensor does not generate a useful voltage signal without supplemental heating. For this reason, the oxygen sensor has a heater to warm the zirconium element, and the engine control computer regulates the supplemental heating using a duty cycle approach to adjust the average current in the zirconium element.</ptxt>
<figure>
<graphic graphicname="A131740J03" width="7.106578999in" height="3.779676365in"/>
</figure>
</item>
</list1>
