<test1>
<ptxt>Start the engine and maintain 2500r/min for 90 seconds to warm up the air fuel ratio sensor (A/F sensor).</ptxt>
</test1>
<test1>
<ptxt>Using SST (TaSCAN), check that the air fuel ratio sensor (A/F sensor) output voltage is normal.</ptxt>
</test1>
<test1>
<ptxt>Shift into 1 gear.</ptxt>
</test1>
<test1>
<ptxt>Accelerate to a vehicle speed of 60km/h, and check that the air fuel ratio sensor (A/F sensor) output voltage rises when the accelerator pedal is released (when deceleration fuel cut is performed).</ptxt>
<list1 type="unordered">
<item>
<ptxt>If the air fuel ratio sensor (A/F sensor) output voltage is fixed at 3.3V, the circuit may be open.</ptxt>
</item>
<item>
<ptxt>If the air fuel ratio sensor (A/F sensor) output voltage is 3.8V or more or 2.8V or less, the circuit may be shorted.</ptxt>
</item>
<item>
<ptxt>When the engine control computer performs deceleration fuel cut, the air-fuel ratio becomes lean and the air fuel ratio sensor (A/F sensor) voltage rises.</ptxt>
</item>
<item>
<ptxt>The engine control computer learns the fully closed throttle position for fuel cut. If the battery terminal is disconnected, the learned value is cancelled, so it is necessary to drive at 16km/h or more to allow it to be learned.</ptxt>
</item>
<item>
<ptxt>During acceleration, etc., the engine control computer increases the fuel volume, so the air fuel ratio sensor (A/F sensor) output voltage may be 2.8V or less.</ptxt>
</item>
<item>
<ptxt>As the air fuel ratio sensor (A/F sensor) uses a current detection element, there is no voltage change even if the terminal voltage of the engine control computer is measured. The voltage displayed on SST (TaSCAN) is the current output by the air fuel ratio sensor (A/F sensor) converted to voltage.</ptxt>
<figure>
<graphic graphicname="A083001J07" width="7.106578999in" height="2.775699831in"/>
</figure>
</item>
</list1>
<spec>
<title>Result</title>
<table>
<tgroup cols="2" align="center">
<colspec colname="COL1" colwidth="2.07in"/>
<colspec colname="COL2" colwidth="2.06in"/>
<thead>
<row>
<entry valign="middle">
<ptxt>Result</ptxt>
</entry>
<entry valign="middle">
<ptxt>Proceed to</ptxt>
</entry>
</row>
</thead>
<tbody>
<row>
<entry valign="middle">
<ptxt>NG</ptxt>
</entry>
<entry valign="middle">
<ptxt>A</ptxt>
</entry>
</row>
<row>
<entry valign="middle">
<ptxt>OK</ptxt>
</entry>
<entry valign="middle">
<ptxt>B</ptxt>
</entry>
</row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</table>
</spec>
</test1>
</content6>
<res>
<down ref="RM0000014C700DX_10_0025" fin="false">A</down>
<right ref="RM0000014C700DX_10_0029" fin="false">B</right>
</res>
